QB.add(15,[{"id":15001,"t":"Tissue Types","s":"A punch biopsy of skin shows many layers, flat surface cells and a tough waterproof surface. Name this epithelium in full.","e":"Skin: stratified squamous keratinised epithelium (many layers, flat surface cells, keratin layer for waterproofing). The oesophagus has the non-keratinised form.","k":"Skin = stratified squamous keratinised.","img":{"src":"15/skin","cap":"Skin surface, H&amp;E."},"a":["stratified squamous keratinised","keratinised stratified squamous","stratified squamous keratinized","keratinized stratified squamous","stratified squamous keratinised epithelium","keratinised stratified squamous epithelium"],"rn":[["","Where is keratinised stratified squamous epithelium?","Where is keratinised stratified squamous epithelium?","Skin"],["","Image card: What type of epithelium is this? Where is it from?","What type of epithelium is this? Where is it from?","Stratified squamous epithelium, keratinised; Skin"],["","Where is non-keratinised stratified squamous epithelium?","Where is non-keratinised stratified squamous epithelium?","Oesophagus"]]},{"id":15002,"t":"Histology Techniques","s":"A pathologist examines a tumour biopsy and comments on the likely course of the disease. Which of the listed uses of histopathology does this illustrate?","o":["Predicting prognosis and disease progression","Comparing normal and diseased microanatomy","Identifying disease features at tissue and cell level","Linking microscopic changes with symptoms","Choosing the right embedding medium"],"e":"The slide lists five uses of histopathology: <b>compare</b> normal vs disease microanatomy (same vs different); <b>identify</b> disease features at tissue and cell level; <b>link</b> with symptoms and mechanism of disease; <b>evaluate</b> courses of treatment; <b>predict</b> prognosis and disease progression (C-I-L-E-P). Commenting on the likely course of disease is prediction of prognosis/progression.","w":["A use, but not what the comment shows.","A use, but not prognosis.","A use, but not prognosis.","Lab process."],"k":"Histopathology: compare, identify, link, evaluate, predict.","rn":[["","What are the 5 uses of histology and histopathology?","What are the 5 uses of histology and histopathology?","Compare normal vs diseased tissue; Identify disease features at tissue and cell level; Link with symptoms and mechanism; Evaluate courses of treatment; Predict prognosis and progression"]]},{"id":15003,"t":"Histology Techniques","s":"Why does a clinician need histopathology even after a full history and chemical tests?","o":["Tests are indirect; tissue shows the actual cellular change","History and tests are unreliable, so tissue replaces them entirely","History and tests are direct; tissue adds only legal evidence","Chemical tests are never accurate for disease","Patients cannot describe their symptoms reliably"],"e":"From the recording: a clinician only gets an <b>abstract description</b> of disease from how the patient experiences it (pain, discomfort) or <b>indirect information</b> from measurable tests (e.g. chemical tests). Histopathology identifies <b>what changes have happened in the tissue</b>, how the disease emerged, how it may progress and the best treatment option.","w":["They are indirect, not replaced.","Indirect.","Not stated.","Not stated."],"k":"Tissue shows directly what history/tests show indirectly.","rn":[["","What information does a clinician get without histology?","What information does a clinician get without histology?","Abstract description of symptoms (pain, discomfort); Indirect information from tests"]]},{"id":15004,"t":"Histology Techniques","s":"Which pairing of term and definition is correct?","o":["Cytology – cells; histology – tissues","Cytology – tissues; histology – cells","Cytology – cells; histology – organs","Cytology – organs; histology – tissues","Cytology – normal cells; histopathology – normal tissue"],"e":"<b>Histology/histopathology</b> = study of <b>tissues</b>; <b>cytology/cytopathology</b> = study of <b>cells</b>. In the recording, histology refers to normal tissue and histopathology to diseased tissue, and microanatomy is used as the same term as histology. In cytology cells are <b>smeared on a slide</b>, stained and studied for abnormalities.","w":["Swapped.","Tissues.","Cells.","Histopathology is disease."],"k":"Histology = tissues; cytology = cells.","rn":[["","Histology","Histology","Study of tissues; Normal tissue"],["","How is cytology done?","How is cytology done?","Cells smeared on a slide; Stained; Studied for abnormalities"],["","Cytology","Cytology","Study of cells; Also called cytopathology"]]},{"id":15005,"t":"Histology Techniques","s":"Which sequence lists the histology/cytology process in the correct order?","o":["Collection → preservation → preparation → staining → imaging","Collection → preparation → preservation → staining → imaging","Collection → preservation → staining → preparation → imaging","Preservation → collection → preparation → staining → imaging","Collection → staining → preservation → preparation → imaging"],"e":"The five steps in order: sample <b>collection</b>, sample <b>preservation</b> (fixation), sample <b>preparation</b> (embedding and sectioning), sample <b>staining</b>, <b>imaging and analysis</b>. Memory aid: “Collect, Preserve, Prepare, Stain, Image”. Staining before fixation would be pointless, as an unfixed sample does not stain properly.","w":["Preserve first.","Prepare before staining.","Collect first.","Preserve before staining."],"k":"Collect → Preserve → Prepare → Stain → Image.","rn":[["","What are the 5 steps of the histology/cytology process?","What are the 5 steps of the histology/cytology process?","Sample collection; Sample preservation; Sample preparation; Sample staining; Imaging and analysis"]]},{"id":15006,"t":"Histology Techniques","s":"A patient has a palpable fluid-filled lump. Name the needle sampling method used.","e":"Fine needle aspiration (needle biopsy) collects cells or fluid from a lump.","k":"Fine needle aspiration.","a":["fine needle aspiration","fna","needle aspiration","needle biopsy","fine-needle aspiration"],"rn":[["","Fine Needle Aspiration is an example of what?","Fine Needle Aspiration is an example of what?","Needle biopsy"],["","FNA","FNA","Fine Needle Aspiration"]]},{"id":15007,"t":"Histology Techniques","s":"A small tool removes a core of all skin layers from the forearm for diagnosis. Name this sampling method.","e":"Punch biopsy: a core of skin layers for skin investigation.","k":"Punch biopsy.","a":["punch biopsy","punch"],"rn":[["","What is a punch biopsy used for?","What is a punch biopsy used for?","Skin investigation"],["","How does a punch biopsy work?","How does a punch biopsy work?","Little tool punctures the skin; Removes a tissue sample"]]},{"id":15008,"t":"Tissue Types","s":"An oesophageal biopsy shows many layers with flat, nucleated surface cells and no keratin. Name this epithelium in full.","e":"Oesophagus: stratified squamous non-keratinised epithelium; named after the shape of its surface cells (squamous) even though basal cells are rounder.","k":"Oesophagus = stratified squamous non-keratinised.","img":{"src":"15/oesoph","cap":"Lining of a tube in the upper GI tract, H&amp;E."},"a":["stratified squamous non-keratinised","non-keratinised stratified squamous","stratified squamous nonkeratinised","stratified squamous non-keratinized","non-keratinized stratified squamous","nonkeratinised stratified squamous"],"rn":[["","Where is non-keratinised stratified squamous epithelium?","Where is non-keratinised stratified squamous epithelium?","Oesophagus"],["","Image card: What type of epithelium is this? Where is it from?","What type of epithelium is this? Where is it from?","Stratified squamous epithelium, non-keratinised; Oesophagus"]]},{"id":15009,"t":"Histology Techniques","s":"Name the fixative that cross-links proteins with a CH₂ bridge, releasing water.","e":"Formaldehyde fixes tissue by cross-linking proteins; it is carcinogenic, so handle with care.","k":"Formaldehyde.","a":["formaldehyde","formalin","methanal"],"rn":[["","What does formaldehyde cross-linking release?","What does formaldehyde cross-linking release?","Water (H2O)"],["","How does formaldehyde work?","How does formaldehyde work?","Cross-links proteins together; Sticks proteins together"]]},{"id":15010,"t":"Histology Techniques","f":"ct10","s":"A biopsy returns from the lab looking pale and washed out, with no recognisable structure. What is the most likely consequence for the patient?","o":["The sample may be useless, risking a repeat biopsy","The sample can be re-fixed after staining with no effect","Only the colour is affected; the diagnosis is unchanged","The diagnosis will be more accurate, as the lighter stain shows more detail","Restaining with eosin will restore the structure"],"e":"<b>Insufficient fixation</b> makes the slide <b>very difficult</b> to study and can lead to an <b>incomplete diagnosis</b>. In the recording: tissue not fixed properly <b>will not stain properly</b>, so it cannot be analysed; the patient may need to <b>come back for another biopsy</b>, which depending on the type is sometimes not a good option.","w":["Not.","Diagnosis affected.","Insufficient fixation harms diagnosis.","Not."],"k":"Poor fixation → poor staining → incomplete diagnosis.","rn":[["","What does a poorly fixed sample mean for the patient?","What does a poorly fixed sample mean for the patient?","Must come back for another biopsy; Sometimes not a good option"],["","Is a poorly fixed sample useful?","Is a poorly fixed sample useful?","No, it is useless"]]},{"id":15011,"t":"Histology Techniques","f":"ct11","s":"Which statement about embedding and sectioning is correct?","o":["Paraffin-embedded, cut up to 10 µm on a microtome; cryotome uses other media","Paraffin-embedded, cut up to 100 µm on a microtome; cryotome uses paraffin","Water-embedded, cut up to 10 µm on a microtome; cryotome uses paraffin","Paraffin-embedded, cut before fixation on a cryotome","Paraffin-embedded, thicker sections give better diagnosis"],"e":"<b>Paraffin embedding</b> of the <b>dehydrated</b> sample; sectioning <b>up to 10 μm</b> with a <b>microtome</b> or a <b>cryotome</b> (embedding medium <b>not paraffin</b>). In the recording, thickness is a maximum of 10 μm and minimum of about 7–8 μm, to achieve <b>single cell layer</b> sections.","w":["10 µm; not paraffin.","Paraffin.","After fixation.","Thin."],"k":"Paraffin, microtome, ≤10 μm; cryotome non-paraffin.","rn":[["","What is different about cryotome sectioning?","What is different about cryotome sectioning?","Embedding medium is not paraffin"],["","What 2 instruments cut tissue sections?","What 2 instruments cut tissue sections?","Microtome; Cryotome"]]},{"id":15012,"t":"Histology Techniques","s":"A technician cuts sections at 25 μm by mistake. Why is this a problem for diagnosis?","o":["Cells overlap, hiding changes in single cells","Cells become unfixed when cut too thick","Stain cannot penetrate any nucleus","Thick sections cannot be imaged by the microscope at all","The section becomes a cytology smear"],"e":"Correct section thickness (≤10 μm) ensures <b>single cell layer</b> sections for correct disease pathology diagnosis. The lecturer explained you want to identify <b>changes in individual cells</b>; with <b>overlapping cell layers</b> it is very difficult to understand what is happening.","w":["Not.","Not stated.","Not the problem.","Not."],"k":"Thin sections → single cell layer → accurate diagnosis.","rn":[["","Why do you want single cell layers?","Why do you want single cell layers?","Identify changes in individual cells; Overlapping layers are hard to interpret"],["","Why is correct embedding and thickness important?","Why is correct embedding and thickness important?","Single cell layer sections; For correct disease pathology diagnosis"]]},{"id":15013,"t":"Histology Techniques","s":"Where are samples processed?","o":["Pathology labs with technicians; Kent mostly Dartford or Maidstone","Pathology labs with technicians; Kent mostly Canterbury or Ashford","University labs run by medical students","GP surgeries with a microscope","The ward, by the clinician who took the sample"],"e":"Fixation, embedding and sectioning happen in <b>pathology labs</b>, done by <b>dedicated technicians</b>. Bigger trusts have their own pathology departments; others send samples – generally from Kent to <b>Dartford</b> or <b>Maidstone</b>.","w":["Dartford/Maidstone.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Processing: pathology labs (Dartford/Maidstone).","rn":[["","Who does tissue processing?","Who does tissue processing?","Dedicated technicians in pathology labs"],["","Where are Kent samples generally sent?","Where are Kent samples generally sent?","Dartford or Maidstone"]]},{"id":15014,"t":"Histology Techniques","s":"A pathologist sees something unusual on an H&amp;E slide and wants to see the tissue layers more clearly. What is the most appropriate next step?","o":["Request a specific stain such as trichrome","Repeat the biopsy, as H&E is unsuitable here","Re-image the slide without any stain","Switch to cytology of the same tissue","Repeat H&E at higher concentration"],"e":"H&E <b>always happens first</b> (now normally automated). Other stains (<b>trichrome</b>, <b>Golgi</b>, <b>silver</b>, specific stains for cartilage, bone, adipose) are <b>more specific and laborious</b>, used under specific instructions – e.g. the pathologist saw something on H&E and wants more detail. Trichrome stains the <b>different layers</b> of a tissue much more clearly. <b>The choice of stain depends on the aim</b> of the analysis.","w":["Not needed.","Not.","Not.","Not stated."],"k":"H&E first; specific stains for specific questions.","rn":[["","When might a pathologist request a special stain?","When might a pathologist request a special stain?","Want to see something specific; Saw something on H&E and want more detail"],["","Why are other stains only used on request?","Why are other stains only used on request?","More specific; More laborious"],["","What are the 5 types of stain listed?","What are the 5 types of stain listed?","H&E; Trichrome stain; Golgi stain; Silver stain; Other specific tissue stains"]]},{"id":15015,"t":"Histology Techniques","f":"ct12","s":"Which statement about how staining is performed is correct?","o":["Manual or automated; H&E now usually automated","Manual or automated high-throughput; H&E is always done manually","Always manual, using staining dishes","Automated only for silver stains","Done before fixation"],"e":"Staining is by <b>manual staining</b> (rack of slides in a staining dish) or <b>automated high-throughput staining</b>. The recording says H&E is now normally <b>automated</b>.","w":["Usually automated.","Both.","Not.","After."],"k":"Manual vs automated staining.","rn":[["","Is H&E staining manual or automated now?","Is H&E staining manual or automated now?","Normally automated"]]},{"id":15016,"t":"Histology Techniques","f":"ct13","s":"On an H&E-stained breast carcinoma, which components are purple and which pink?","o":["Purple: DNA and nuclei; pink: cytoplasm and matrix","Purple: cytoplasm and matrix; pink: DNA and nuclei","Purple: mucus and lipid; pink: DNA and nuclei","Purple: collagen fibres; pink: nuclei and nucleoli","Purple: nuclei and cytoplasm; pink: matrix only"],"e":"<b>Haematoxylin</b> gives <b>purple</b> (DNA and nuclei); <b>eosin</b> gives <b>pink</b> (cytoplasm and extracellular matrix). The other example on the slide is skin with a <b>trichrome</b> stain (an acid combination stain).","w":["Swapped.","Unstained.","Not.","Cytoplasm pink."],"k":"H&E colours.","rn":[["","Why is anything purple a nucleus?","Why is anything purple a nucleus?","DNA stains purple with haematoxylin"],["","What is the exception to haematoxylin staining nuclei?","What is the exception to haematoxylin staining nuclei?","Cartilage; Whole tissue stains purple (carbohydrates)"]]},{"id":15017,"t":"Histology Techniques","s":"Haematoxylin is a basic dye. Which structure would it stain, and why?","o":["The nucleolus, as nucleic acid is acidic (basophilic)","The nucleolus, as nucleic acid is basic (acidophilic)","Cytoplasmic proteins, as they are basophilic","Collagen fibres, as they are acidic","Lipid droplets, as they are acidic"],"e":"<b>Haematoxylin</b> (basic) stains <b>acidic/basophilic</b> structures: <b>DNA (nucleus, nucleolus), RNA</b> and <b>carbohydrates</b> (cartilage molecules). <b>Eosin</b> (acidic) stains <b>basic/acidophilic</b> structures: cytoplasmic proteins and extracellular fibres. Opposites attract.","w":["Acidic.","Acidophilic.","Pink.","Unstained."],"k":"Haematoxylin → nucleic acids.","rn":[["","Haematoxylin","Haematoxylin","Basic dye; Stains acidic (basophilic) structures; Purple"]]},{"id":15018,"t":"Histology Techniques","s":"A tissue section stains purple throughout, not just its nuclei. Which tissue is this exception, and why?","o":["Cartilage, as its carbohydrates stain with haematoxylin","Cartilage, as its collagen stains with haematoxylin","Tendon, as its collagen is basophilic","Adipose tissue, as lipid stains purple","Smooth muscle, as its proteins are basophilic"],"e":"The general rule is that purple = nucleus. A small, specific <b>exception</b> is <b>cartilage</b>: the <b>whole tissue stains purple</b> because of its <b>carbohydrates</b> (haematoxylin also stains carbohydrates).","w":["Carbohydrates.","Pink.","Unstained.","Pink."],"k":"Cartilage stains purple (carbohydrates).","rn":[["","What is the exception to haematoxylin staining nuclei?","What is the exception to haematoxylin staining nuclei?","Cartilage; Whole tissue stains purple (carbohydrates)"]]},{"id":15019,"t":"Tissue Types","s":"On H&E, adipocytes and goblet cells both contain large empty-looking spaces. What explains this?","o":["Lipid and mucus do not stain, so look empty","Lipid and mucus are destroyed by fixation","Eosin bleaches lipid and mucus","They contain only nuclei","They are sectioning artefacts"],"e":"From the recording: if something is <b>not protein, not DNA and not carbohydrate, it will not stain</b>. <b>Mucus</b> and <b>lipid</b> therefore appear as <b>empty areas</b>. Goblet cells look like they contain an <b>empty balloon</b> because mucus does not stain.","w":["Not.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Mucus and lipid don't stain → empty spaces.","rn":[["","Why do goblet cells look empty?","Why do goblet cells look empty?","Mucus does not stain with H&E"],["","What do goblet cells look like on H&E?","What do goblet cells look like on H&E?","Empty balloon; Mucus does not stain"],["","How does mucus appear on H&E?","How does mucus appear on H&E?","Does not stain; Looks empty"]]},{"id":15020,"t":"Histology Techniques","s":"Why is H&E, despite not showing organelles, “fine enough” for histopathology?","o":["A large resource of normal images allows comparison","It shows all organelles at high magnification","It is the cheapest stain, which is all that matters","Diseases do not change how cells look","Pathologists never need other stains"],"e":"H&E seems basic (no organelles or fine details) but is <b>fine enough</b> to identify changes in cells, because there is a <b>large resource of normal images</b> to compare against what is thought to be abnormal.","w":["Not.","Not the reason.","They do.","They do."],"k":"H&E works via comparison with normal.","rn":[["","Why is H&E good enough for histopathology?","Why is H&E good enough for histopathology?","Large resource of what normal looks like; Compare normal with abnormal"]]},{"id":15021,"t":"Histology Techniques","f":"ct15","s":"Which statement about sample imaging is correct?","o":["The microscope is still the gold standard; WSI aids archiving","WSI has replaced the microscope as the gold standard","The microscope is still the gold standard; WSI lowers resolution","Glass slides must be kept forever, so WSI is unused","Histopathologists are technicians who run scanners"],"e":"Imaging: <b>microscope</b> (light, fluorescent), <b>scanner</b>, and <b>WSI</b> (high resolution imaging, digitalisation, virtual slide analysis). In histopathology the <b>gold standard is still the microscope</b>. The histopathologist is <b>not a technician</b> – histopathology is a sub-specialty of medicine. Virtual microscopy helps <b>storage and archiving</b>, removing the need to keep glass slides long term.","w":["Microscope.","High resolution.","Archiving.","Doctors."],"k":"Microscope = gold standard; WSI for archiving.","rn":[["","What is the gold standard in histopathology?","What is the gold standard in histopathology?","Still the microscope"]]},{"id":15022,"t":"Histology Techniques","f":"ct17","s":"At low magnification, which findings can you identify on a slide?","o":["General organisation, layers and large changes","Individual nucleoli and chromatin detail","Mitotic figures in individual cells","Mucin vesicles in single cells","Ribosomes and other organelles"],"e":"<b>Low magnification</b> shows the <b>different layers</b> and larger structures – the general organisation and <b>large changes</b> such as <b>infiltration</b> of other tissue or <b>big growths</b>. Then zoom in: identify <b>structures</b>, then <b>cell types</b>. Some prefer starting at high magnification; it is personal choice.","w":["High power.","High power.","High power.","Not visible."],"k":"Low power = organisation and big changes.","rn":[["","What does low magnification show?","What does low magnification show?","General structure and organisation; Large changes"]]},{"id":15029,"t":"The Cell","f":"ct32","s":"Which cell components can actually be identified in a routine histology section?","o":["Cell membrane, cytoplasm, nucleus and nucleolus","Cell membrane, mitochondria, nucleus and nucleolus","Golgi apparatus, ribosomes and nucleus","Microtubules, microfilaments and cytoplasm","Centrioles, smooth ER and nucleus"],"e":"In histology you can identify the <b>cell membrane</b>, <b>cytoplasm</b>, <b>nucleus/nucleoplasm (chromatin)</b>; the H&E image also shows the <b>nuclear membrane</b> and <b>nucleolus</b>. The detailed organelles in diagrams (Golgi, mitochondria, ER, ribosomes, lysosomes, microtubules, centrioles) are not seen.","w":["Mitochondria not seen.","Not seen.","Not seen.","Not seen."],"k":"Visible: membrane, cytoplasm, nucleus, nucleolus.","rn":[["","What 4 things can be identified in a cell in histology?","What 4 things can be identified in a cell in histology?","Cell membrane; Cytoplasm; Nucleus / nucleoplasm; Chromatin"],["","What 3 parts of the nucleus can be seen?","What 3 parts of the nucleus can be seen?","Nuclear membrane; Nucleoplasm; Nucleolus"]]},{"id":15030,"t":"The Cell","s":"In H&amp;E-stained cells, a dark dot is seen inside each purple nucleus. Name it.","e":"The nucleolus is a dark dot within the nucleus; histology identifies the cell membrane, cytoplasm, nucleus and nucleolus, not other organelles.","k":"Nucleolus.","a":["nucleolus","nucleoli"],"rn":[["","Name the parts of the nucleus.","Name the parts of the nucleus.","Nucleolus; Nuclear membrane; Chromosomes / DNA"],["","What 3 parts of the nucleus can be seen?","What 3 parts of the nucleus can be seen?","Nuclear membrane; Nucleoplasm; Nucleolus"]]},{"id":15031,"t":"The Cell","s":"A cell shows dark condensed chromosomes lined up in the middle. Name the mitotic stage.","e":"Metaphase: chromosomes gather in the middle of the cell.","k":"Metaphase.","a":["metaphase"],"rn":[["","Image card: What stage of mitosis is this?","What stage of mitosis is this?","Metaphase"]]},{"id":15032,"t":"The Cell","f":"ct35","s":"A cell has two dark chromosome groups moving to opposite poles. What comes next?","o":["Telophase, with two nuclei forming","Metaphase, with chromosomes in the middle","Prophase, with chromosomes condensing","DNA replication, with grainy chromatin","Apoptosis, with apoptotic bodies"],"e":"Two groups pulling apart = <b>anaphase</b>; next is <b>telophase</b> (diagram H), where <b>two separate nuclei</b> form.","w":["Before.","Earlier.","Before division.","Not."],"k":"Anaphase → telophase."},{"id":15033,"t":"The Cell","f":"ct36","s":"What is the first step of apoptosis shown?","o":["Microvilli contract, junctions break, chromatin starts condensing","The cell shrinks and chromatin condenses at the nuclear rim","The cell blebs violently","The cell fragments into apoptotic bodies","Macrophages phagocytose the cell"],"e":"Apoptosis: (1) <b>microvilli contract, junctions break, chromatin begins to condense</b>; (2) cell <b>shrinks</b>, chromatin condenses at the nuclear periphery; (3) cell <b>blebs violently</b>; (4) <b>fragments</b> into membrane-enclosed apoptotic bodies; (5) apoptotic bodies <b>phagocytosed</b> by neighbouring cells and roving macrophages. Memory aid: Detach → Shrink → Bleb → Fragment → Eaten.","w":["Step 2.","Step 3.","Step 4.","Later."],"k":"Apoptosis step 1: detach.","rn":[["","Apoptosis (cell death) step 1: what happens?","Apoptosis (cell death) step 1: what happens?","Microvilli contract; Intercellular junctions break; Chromatin begins to condense"]]},{"id":15034,"t":"The Cell","s":"On H&E, a small, shrunken cell with very condensed chromatin is seen among normal cells. What is the best interpretation?","o":["A cell undergoing apoptosis","A cell in telophase","A goblet cell","A fibroblast","A cell undergoing fixation artefact only"],"e":"<b>Shrunken cells</b> are examples of cell death; in apoptosis the cell <b>shrinks</b> and chromatin <b>condenses</b> around the nuclear periphery, then blebs and fragments.","w":["Two nuclei.","Pale mucus-filled.","Elongated nuclei.","Shrinkage with condensed chromatin is apoptosis."],"k":"Shrunken + condensed chromatin = apoptosis.","rn":[["","Apoptosis step 2: what happens?","Apoptosis step 2: what happens?","Cell shrinks; Chromatin condenses around the nuclear edge"],["","Apoptosis step 3: what happens?","Apoptosis step 3: what happens?","Cell blebs violently; Chromatin condensation continues"]]},{"id":15035,"t":"The Cell","s":"Name the roving immune cells that, with neighbouring cells, remove apoptotic bodies.","e":"Apoptotic bodies are removed by neighbouring cells and macrophages.","k":"Macrophages.","a":["macrophages","macrophage"],"rn":[["","Apoptosis step 5: what happens to apoptotic bodies?","Apoptosis step 5: what happens to apoptotic bodies?","Phagocytosed by neighbouring cells; And by roving macrophages"]]},{"id":15036,"t":"Tissue Types","s":"Which of the following is <b>not</b> one of the four types of human tissue?","o":["Glandular tissue","Epithelial tissue","Connective tissue","Muscle tissue","Nervous tissue"],"e":"The four tissue types: <b>epithelial, connective, muscle, nervous</b>. Glands are built from epithelium.","w":["A type.","A type.","A type.","A type."],"k":"Four tissue types.","rn":[["","What are the 4 types of human tissue?","What are the 4 types of human tissue?","Epithelial; Connective; Muscle; Nervous"]]},{"id":15037,"t":"Tissue Types","f":"ct40","s":"One image shows a single layer of flat cells with flat nuclei; another a single layer of cube-shaped cells with round nuclei. How are they classified?","o":["Simple squamous; simple cuboidal","Stratified squamous; simple columnar","Simple cuboidal; transitional","Pseudostratified; simple squamous","Endothelium; stratified cuboidal"],"e":"Epithelium is classified by <b>shape</b> (squamous, cuboidal, columnar) and <b>layers</b> (simple, stratified, pseudostratified, transitional). One layer of flat cells = <b>simple squamous</b>; one layer of cube-shaped cells with round nuclei = <b>simple cuboidal</b>.","w":["Single layer, not stratified.","Not transitional.","Not pseudostratified.","Not stratified."],"k":"Shape + layers.","rn":[["","Image card: What type of epithelium is this? How can you tell?","What type of epithelium is this? How can you tell?","Simple squamous epithelium; Single layer of flat cells; N: nuclei"],["","Image card: What type of epithelium is this? How can you tell?","What type of epithelium is this? How can you tell?","Simple cuboidal epithelium; Single layer of cube-like cells; N: nuclei"],["","What are the 3 epithelial cell shapes?","What are the 3 epithelial cell shapes?","Squamous; Cuboidal; Columnar"]]},{"id":15038,"t":"Tissue Types","f":"ct41","s":"In which two organs was simple squamous epithelium shown?","o":["Glomerulus and cornea","Skin and oesophagus","Bladder and urethra","Trachea and bronchi","Pancreas and colon"],"e":"Simple squamous epithelium examples: <b>kidney glomerulus</b> and <b>cornea</b>. In the kidney you can see a nucleus but not really the cytoplasm, though it is there; the cornea shows a single layer.","w":["Stratified.","Transitional.","Pseudostratified.","Not."],"k":"Simple squamous: glomerulus, cornea.","rn":[["","Where is simple squamous epithelium found (2 examples)?","Where is simple squamous epithelium found (2 examples)?","Kidney glomerulus; Cornea"]]},{"id":15039,"t":"Tissue Types","s":"Pale cells that look like empty balloons sit among tall columnar cells in the gut lining. Name them.","e":"Goblet cells contain mucus, which does not stain on H&amp;E, so they look empty.","k":"Goblet cells.","a":["goblet cells","goblet cell","goblet"],"rn":[["","What do goblet cells look like on H&E?","What do goblet cells look like on H&E?","Empty balloon; Mucus does not stain"],["","Why do goblet cells look empty?","Why do goblet cells look empty?","Mucus does not stain with H&E"],["","Image card: What are the cells marked G? How can you tell?","What are the cells marked G? How can you tell?","Goblet cells; Mucus-producing cells in columnar epithelium; Look like empty balloons: mucus does not stain"]]},{"id":15040,"t":"Tissue Types","f":"ct43","s":"Which organ was used to show both simple cuboidal and simple columnar epithelium?","o":["Pancreas","Cornea","Skin","Bladder","Oesophagus"],"e":"The <b>pancreas</b> image shows both <b>simple cuboidal</b> and <b>simple columnar</b> epithelium.","w":["Simple squamous.","Stratified keratinised.","Transitional.","Stratified non-keratinised."],"k":"Pancreas: cuboidal and columnar.","rn":[["","Image card: What type of epithelium is the arrow pointing to? Which organ?","What type of epithelium is the arrow pointing to? Which organ?","Simple cuboidal epithelium; Pancreas"],["","Image card: What type of epithelium is the arrow pointing to? Which organ?","What type of epithelium is the arrow pointing to? Which organ?","Simple columnar epithelium; Pancreas"],["","Where is simple cuboidal epithelium found (example)?","Where is simple cuboidal epithelium found (example)?","Pancreas"]]},{"id":15041,"t":"Tissue Types","f":"ct44","s":"A stratified squamous epithelium is described with layers S, M and B. What do these stand for?","o":["Superficial, middle, basal","Squamous, mucous, basement","Simple, multiple, branched","Stratified, mixed, basophilic","Surface, muscle, blood"],"e":"<b>S / M / B</b> = <b>superficial / middle / basal</b> layers. In stratified epithelium the name comes from the shape of the <b>superficial</b> layer.","w":["Not.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"S/M/B = superficial/middle/basal.","rn":[["","What are the 3 layers of stratified squamous epithelium?","What are the 3 layers of stratified squamous epithelium?","S: superficial; M: middle; B: basal"],["","Image card: What type of epithelium is this? What are S, M and B?","What type of epithelium is this? What are S, M and B?","Stratified squamous epithelium; S: superficial; M: middle; B: basal"],["","What are the 3 layers of transitional epithelium?","What are the 3 layers of transitional epithelium?","S: superficial; M: middle; B: basal"]]},{"id":15042,"t":"Tissue Types","s":"A stratified epithelium has flattened cells at the surface but rounder cells at its base. How should it be named?","o":["Stratified squamous – named from the surface layer","Stratified cuboidal – named from the basal layer","Pseudostratified – named because shapes differ","Transitional – named because shape changes","Simple squamous – named from the surface layer"],"e":"For stratified epithelium the name comes from the shape of the <b>superficial (S)</b> layer, so flat surface cells = <b>stratified squamous</b>. Name with two words: layers + shape.","w":["Surface.","Not.","Not.","Layered."],"k":"Stratified: named by superficial layer.","rn":[["","What are the 3 layers of stratified squamous epithelium?","What are the 3 layers of stratified squamous epithelium?","S: superficial; M: middle; B: basal"]]},{"id":15043,"t":"Tissue Types","f":"ct45","s":"Which pairing of stratified squamous epithelium and organ is correct?","o":["Keratinised – skin; non-keratinised – oesophagus","Keratinised – oesophagus; non-keratinised – skin","Keratinised – skin; non-keratinised – bladder","Keratinised – skin; non-keratinised – cornea","Keratinised – pancreas; non-keratinised – oesophagus"],"e":"Stratified squamous <b>keratinised</b> from <b>skin</b>; stratified squamous from the <b>oesophagus</b> (<b>non-keratinised</b>). The slide caption is worded confusingly but the bracket gives the meaning: non-keratinised.","w":["Swapped.","Transitional.","Simple squamous.","Not."],"k":"K skin; NK oesophagus.","rn":[["","Where is non-keratinised stratified squamous epithelium?","Where is non-keratinised stratified squamous epithelium?","Oesophagus"],["","Image card: What type of epithelium is this? Where is it from?","What type of epithelium is this? Where is it from?","Stratified squamous epithelium, non-keratinised; Oesophagus"]]},{"id":15044,"t":"Tissue Types","f":"ct46","s":"A duct is lined by two or more layers of cube-shaped cells. What is this epithelium, and where is an example found?","o":["Stratified cuboidal – an oesophageal duct","Simple cuboidal – an oesophageal duct","Stratified cuboidal – a kidney tubule","Transitional – the bladder","Stratified squamous – the oesophagus"],"e":"<b>Stratified cuboidal</b> epithelium (two or more layers of cuboidal cells) lining an <b>oesophagus duct</b>. The recording stresses that cuboidal epithelium <b>can still be stratified</b>.","w":["Two or more layers.","Duct.","Not.","Cube-shaped."],"k":"Stratified cuboidal: oesophageal duct.","rn":[["","Where is stratified cuboidal epithelium found (example)?","Where is stratified cuboidal epithelium found (example)?","An oesophagus duct"],["","Image card: What type of epithelium is the arrow pointing to? Where is it from?","What type of epithelium is the arrow pointing to? Where is it from?","Stratified cuboidal epithelium; An oesophagus duct"]]},{"id":15045,"t":"Tissue Types","s":"A bladder biopsy shows an epithelium with superficial, middle and basal layers that can stretch. Name it.","e":"Transitional epithelium lines the bladder.","k":"Transitional.","a":["transitional","transitional epithelium","urothelium"],"rn":[["","Image card: What type of epithelium is this? What are S, M and B?","What type of epithelium is this? What are S, M and B?","Transitional epithelium; S: superficial; M: middle; B: basal"],["","What are the 3 layers of transitional epithelium?","What are the 3 layers of transitional epithelium?","S: superficial; M: middle; B: basal"],["","Image card: What type of epithelium do the arrows point to? Where is it from?","What type of epithelium do the arrows point to? Where is it from?","Transitional epithelium; Bladder"]]},{"id":15046,"t":"Tissue Types","s":"Airway pseudostratified epithelium has an additional apical surface feature. Which?","o":["Cilia","Keratin","Microvilli","Goblet cells only","Smooth muscle"],"e":"The lecturer described it as <b>pseudostratified columnar epithelium with cilia</b>. Pseudostratified epithelium looks layered because nuclei are at different heights.","w":["Skin.","Gut.","Not the feature.","Not."],"k":"Pseudostratified columnar ciliated.","rn":[["","Image card: What type of epithelium is this? How can you tell?","What type of epithelium is this? How can you tell?","Pseudostratified (columnar) epithelium; With cilia"],["","What is special about pseudostratified epithelium?","What is special about pseudostratified epithelium?","Columnar with cilia"]]},{"id":15047,"t":"Tissue Types","f":"ct48","s":"Which three features help you identify a blood vessel?","o":["Endothelium, surrounding smooth muscle, red cells in the lumen","Endothelium, surrounding skeletal muscle, red cells in the lumen","Keratin, goblet cells and cilia","Collagen, elastic fibres and fibroblasts","Mucin vesicles, nucleoli and microvilli"],"e":"Black arrow: <b>endothelium</b>; blue arrow: surrounding <b>smooth muscle</b>; green arrow: <b>red blood cells</b>. Blood vessels can have surrounding smooth muscle and can be identified by red blood cells.","w":["Smooth.","Not.","Connective.","Not."],"k":"Vessel: endothelium + smooth muscle + RBCs.","rn":[["","Image card: What is this structure? What do the black, blue and green arrows show?","What is this structure? What do the black, blue and green arrows show?","Blood vessel; Black: endothelium; Blue: surrounding smooth muscle; Green: red blood cells"],["","How can blood vessels be identified?","How can blood vessels be identified?","Red blood cells inside; Endothelium lining; Surrounding smooth muscle"]]},{"id":15048,"t":"Tissue Types","s":"A vessel-like space lined by flat cells has no red blood cells inside. Should you exclude a blood vessel?","o":["No – use endothelium and smooth muscle too","No – it must be a lymph node instead","Yes – all vessels contain red cells","Yes – it must be a gland duct","Yes – an empty lumen reflects a fixation error"],"e":"The “And finally” slide warns that sections may show “damage”: <b>vessels don't contain red blood cells</b>, epithelial breaks, overlapping folds/cells, blurred cilia, pale staining. Look for the endothelial lining and surrounding smooth muscle.","w":["Not.","Not always.","Not.","Sections can lack red cells."],"k":"Empty vessels still possible.","rn":[["","How can blood vessels be identified?","How can blood vessels be identified?","Red blood cells inside; Endothelium lining; Surrounding smooth muscle"]]},{"id":15050,"t":"Tissue Types","s":"Which pairing of connective tissue subtype and group is correct?","o":["Reticular – loose fibrous","Elastic – loose fibrous","Areolar – dense fibrous","Hyaline – dense fibrous","Spongy bone – fluid"],"e":"<b>Loose</b> fibrous: <b>areolar, reticular</b>. <b>Dense</b>: regular, irregular, <b>elastic</b>. Hyaline and fibrocartilage are cartilage (specialised); compact and spongy are bone (specialised); blood is fluid.","w":["Dense.","Loose.","Cartilage.","Bone."],"k":"Loose: areolar, reticular.","rn":[["","What are the 2 types of loose connective tissue?","What are the 2 types of loose connective tissue?","Areolar; Reticular"]]},{"id":15051,"t":"Tissue Types","s":"Elongated dark nuclei lie between pink wavy collagen fibres. Name the cells.","e":"Fibroblasts lie among collagen fibres in fibrous connective tissue.","k":"Fibroblasts.","a":["fibroblasts","fibroblast"],"rn":[["","Image card: What does this connective tissue show?","What does this connective tissue show?","Collagen fibres; Fibroblasts (dark nuclei)"],["","Image card: What cells are these, and what is C?","What cells are these, and what is C?","Fibroblasts; C: collagen"],["","What are the 3 components of fibrous tissue?","What are the 3 components of fibrous tissue?","Fibroblasts; Collagen fibres; Elastic fibres"]]},{"id":15052,"t":"Tissue Types","f":"ct51","s":"How do elastic fibres (E) differ from collagen fibres?","o":["Elastic: thin, dark, wavy; collagen: broad pink bundles","Elastic: broad pink bundles; collagen: thin, dark, wavy","Elastic and collagen look identical","Elastic fibres are unstained on H&E","Elastic fibres are purple with nuclei"],"e":"<b>Collagen fibres</b>: broad pink bundles. <b>Elastic fibres (E)</b>: thin, darker, wavy fibres, with a fibroblast (F) marked.","w":["Swapped.","Different.","Stained.","Not."],"k":"Collagen broad; elastic thin, dark.","rn":[["","What are the 3 components of fibrous tissue?","What are the 3 components of fibrous tissue?","Fibroblasts; Collagen fibres; Elastic fibres"],["","Image card: What does this connective tissue show?","What does this connective tissue show?","Collagen fibres; Fibroblasts (dark nuclei)"]]},{"id":15053,"t":"Tissue Types","f":"ct53","s":"Smooth muscle cut lengthways and across look different. Which description is correct?","o":["Lengthways: spindles; across: rounded, central nuclei","Lengthways: striated fibres; across: large multinucleated cells","Lengthways: rounded, central nuclei; across: spindles","Lengthways and across: flat squamous cells","Lengthways: spindles with peripheral nuclei"],"e":"Smooth muscle <b>longitudinal</b>: long <b>spindle-shaped</b> cells with <b>elongated nuclei</b>; <b>transverse</b>: rounded cell profiles with central nuclei. Smooth muscle has no striation.","w":["Smooth muscle is not striated.","Swapped.","Not.","Central."],"k":"Smooth muscle in two planes."},{"id":15054,"t":"Tissue Types","f":"ct55","s":"What is the key feature to notice in cardiac muscle cut longitudinally?","o":["Striation – fine cross-banding","No striation – smooth appearance","Cigar-shaped nuclei only","Goblet cells between fibres","Keratin layers"],"e":"Cardiac muscle longitudinal: “<b>notice the striation</b>” – fine cross-banding running across the fibres, with oval nuclei. Smooth muscle has none.","w":["Striated.","Look for striation.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Cardiac: striated."},{"id":15056,"t":"Tissue Types","f":"ct56","s":"In which organs were nerve ganglia shown, and how are they recognised?","o":["Colon and pancreas; a separate structure from surrounding tissue","Colon and pancreas; a keratinised structure in the epithelium","Skin and bladder; a separate structure from surrounding tissue","Kidney and cornea; flat cells","Heart and lung; striated"],"e":"<b>Nerve ganglia</b> in the <b>colon</b> and <b>pancreas</b>. They are often <b>embedded in connective tissue</b> or among specialised epithelium (pancreas), and look like a <b>separate structure</b> from the surrounding tissue.","w":["Separate.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Ganglia: colon, pancreas; distinct structure.","rn":[["","Image card: What is the arrow pointing to? Which organ?","What is the arrow pointing to? Which organ?","Nerve ganglion; Pancreas; Separate structure from surrounding tissue"],["","How can you identify a nerve ganglion?","How can you identify a nerve ganglion?","Looks like a separate structure from the surrounding tissue"]]},{"id":15057,"t":"Structure and Function","f":"ct58","s":"A cell's cytoplasm is filled with pale vesicles labelled MV. What is its function?","o":["Mucin secretion","Protein secretion","Ion pumping","Contraction","Impulse conduction"],"e":"<b>Mucin secreting</b> epithelial cells: cytoplasm filled with pale <b>mucin vesicles (MV)</b>. The appearance of a cell is linked to its <b>function</b>.","w":["Dark.","Membranes.","Muscle.","Nerve."],"k":"MV = mucin vesicles.","rn":[["","MV","MV","Mucin vesicles"],["","Image card: What type of cells are these? What is MV?","What type of cells are these? What is MV?","Mucin secreting epithelial cells; MV: mucin vesicles"],["","What do mucin secreting cells contain?","What do mucin secreting cells contain?","Mucin vesicles (MV)"]]},{"id":15058,"t":"Structure and Function","f":"ct59","s":"Why do protein-secreting epithelial cells have very dark-stained cytoplasm?","o":["Their high protein content","Their high mucus content","Their high lipid content","Their dying state","Their lack of nuclei"],"e":"From the recording: <b>protein-secreting</b> cells have a <b>very dark stained cytoplasm</b> because of their <b>high protein content</b> (eosin stains protein). Ion-pumping cells have <b>lots of membranes</b> for ion exchange.","w":["Pale.","Pale.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Protein-secreting = dark cytoplasm.","rn":[["","Why is protein secreting cytoplasm dark?","Why is protein secreting cytoplasm dark?","High protein content"]]},{"id":15059,"t":"Structure and Function","s":"Which epithelial cell type has extensive membranes for ion exchange?","o":["Ion-pumping epithelial cells","Mucin-secreting cells","Protein-secreting cells","Goblet cells","Keratinocytes"],"e":"<b>Ion-pumping</b> epithelial cells have <b>lots of membranes</b> and carry out a lot of <b>ion exchange</b>.","w":["Vesicles.","Dark.","Mucus.","Skin."],"k":"Ion pumping: many membranes.","rn":[["","Image card: What type of epithelial cells are these?","What type of epithelial cells are these?","Ion pumping epithelial cells; Lots of membranes, ion exchange"],["","What do ion pumping epithelial cells have?","What do ion pumping epithelial cells have?","Lots of membranes; Lots of ion exchange"]]},{"id":15060,"t":"Summary","s":"Using the seven-step approach, what should you do immediately after identifying tissue types at low magnification?","o":["Increase magnification to confirm cell types","Predict the likely change in function","Decide whether it is abnormal","Find out the organ source","Annotate the slide for revision"],"e":"Seven steps: (1) <b>organ/tissue source</b>; (2) <b>low magnification</b> – tissue types; (3) <b>increase magnification</b> – confirm cell types and arrangement; (4) <b>associations</b> between cell types, arrangement and function; (5) <b>as expected (normal?)</b>; (6) if not, <b>why/what changed?</b>; (7) <b>predicted change in function</b>.","w":["Later step.","Later.","Step 1.","Not a step."],"k":"Step 3: increase magnification.","rn":[["","What do you confirm at higher magnification?","What do you confirm at higher magnification?","Cell types; Arrangement within each tissue"],["","What are the 7 steps to analyse a histology image?","What are the 7 steps to analyse a histology image?","Know the organ/tissue source; Low magnification: tissue types; Higher magnification: cell types and arrangement; Link cells, arrangement and function; Does it look normal?; If not, what has changed?; Predicted change in function"]]},{"id":15061,"t":"Summary","s":"A student sees an abnormal-looking area in a colon section. According to the seven-step approach, which two questions follow once something does not look as expected?","o":["What has changed? Then the change in function","What is the organ source? Then which stain was used on it?","Which collection method? Then which fixative?","Is it cytology? Then is it histology?","What thickness? Then which microtome?"],"e":"After asking whether features look <b>as expected (normal?)</b>: if not, <b>why – what has changed?</b>, and then <b>what is the predicted change in function</b> when microanatomy looks abnormal. This links structure to function and disease.","w":["Step 1, not after abnormality.","Lab.","Not.","Lab."],"k":"Abnormal → what changed → predicted function.","rn":[["","If features look abnormal, what do you ask?","If features look abnormal, what do you ask?","Why?; What has changed?; Predicted change in function"]]},{"id":15062,"t":"Summary","s":"What is the very first step when analysing any histology image?","o":["Find out the organ or tissue source","Zoom to the highest magnification","Identify any mitotic figures","Predict the change in function","Annotate the slide"],"e":"Step 1: information about the <b>organ source or tissue source</b>. The recording also says the first step in analysing anything is to <b>know what you are looking at</b>.","w":["Later.","Later.","Last.","Not."],"k":"Step 1: source.","rn":[["","What is the first thing to know about a histology image?","What is the first thing to know about a histology image?","Organ or tissue source"]]},{"id":15063,"t":"Summary","s":"On an airway slide, the cilia look like a blurred stain and the epithelium appears broken. What is the most appropriate interpretation?","o":["Probably preparation “damage” in a real section","Definite ciliary disease of the airway","Evidence that the tissue is not epithelium at all","Evidence of widespread apoptosis","Evidence of cartilage, not epithelium"],"e":"“And finally”: histology sections may show some <b>“damage”</b>, so cells/tissues may not look as flawless as textbooks: <b>cilia</b> like a blurred stain, <b>breaks</b> in epithelium, <b>overlapping folds</b> or <b>cells</b>, vessels <b>without red blood cells</b>, <b>pale</b> staining.","w":["Not definite.","It is epithelium.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Expect artefacts.","rn":[["","Why may cells not look like the textbook?","Why may cells not look like the textbook?","Histology sections may show damage"]]},{"id":15064,"t":"Histology Techniques","s":"Which of the following is <b>not</b> listed as an example of “damage” in histology sections?","o":["Nuclei stained pink","Epithelial breaks","Overlapping tissue folds","Vessels without red blood cells","Pale staining"],"e":"Listed: blurred cilia, epithelial <b>breaks</b>, overlapping <b>folds</b> and cells, vessels <b>without RBCs</b>, <b>pale</b> staining. Pink nuclei is not listed.","w":["Listed.","Listed.","Listed.","Listed."],"k":"Artefact list.","rn":[["","What colour does eosin stain?","What colour does eosin stain?","Pink"],["","How do protein-rich secretions in ducts stain?","How do protein-rich secretions in ducts stain?","Pink (eosin)"],["","Eosin","Eosin","Acidic dye; Stains basic (acidophilic) structures; Pink"]]},{"id":15066,"t":"Summary","s":"Which description of cytology is correct?","o":["Cells smeared, stained and studied","Tissue embedded in paraffin and sectioned","Whole organs imaged under a scanner","Cells grown in culture and stained","Tissue fixed and cut on a cryotome"],"e":"<b>Cytology</b>: study of cells at the microscopic level – cells <b>smeared on a slide</b>, stained and studied for abnormalities. <b>Histology</b> is a multi-step process for tissues.","w":["Histology.","Not.","Not.","Histology."],"k":"Cytology: smear, stain, study.","rn":[["","How is cytology done?","How is cytology done?","Cells smeared on a slide; Stained; Studied for abnormalities"]]},{"id":15069,"t":"Tissue Types","s":"A skin sample is taken with a punch, fixed, embedded and stained with H&E. The surface layers are flat and anucleate-looking with a waterproof layer. Which epithelium is shown?","o":["Stratified squamous keratinised","Stratified squamous non-keratinised","Transitional","Simple cuboidal","Pseudostratified columnar"],"e":"Skin = <b>stratified squamous keratinised</b> epithelium; the oesophagus is non-keratinised. Punch biopsy is the method for skin investigation.","w":["Mouth/oesophagus.","Bladder.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Skin = K stratified squamous.","rn":[["","Where is keratinised stratified squamous epithelium?","Where is keratinised stratified squamous epithelium?","Skin"],["","Image card: What type of epithelium is this? Where is it from?","What type of epithelium is this? Where is it from?","Stratified squamous epithelium, keratinised; Skin"],["","Image card: What type of epithelium is this? Where is it from?","What type of epithelium is this? Where is it from?","Stratified squamous epithelium, non-keratinised; Oesophagus"]]},{"id":15070,"t":"Tissue Types","s":"A slide shows an oesophagus section with multiple layers of flat surface cells without keratin, and a nearby duct with two layers of cube-shaped cells. Which two epithelia are present?","o":["Stratified squamous non-keratinised; stratified cuboidal","Stratified squamous keratinised; simple cuboidal","Stratified squamous non-keratinised; simple cuboidal","Transitional; stratified cuboidal","Simple squamous; stratified cuboidal"],"e":"Oesophagus: <b>stratified squamous non-keratinised</b>; oesophageal duct: <b>stratified cuboidal</b>.","w":["No keratin; two layers.","Two layers.","Not.","Multiple layers."],"k":"Oesophagus epithelia.","rn":[["","Where is non-keratinised stratified squamous epithelium?","Where is non-keratinised stratified squamous epithelium?","Oesophagus"],["","Image card: What type of epithelium is this? Where is it from?","What type of epithelium is this? Where is it from?","Stratified squamous epithelium, non-keratinised; Oesophagus"]]},{"id":15071,"t":"Tissue Types","s":"Which pairing of epithelium and organ example is <b>incorrect</b>?","o":["Transitional – cornea","Simple squamous – kidney glomerulus","Simple cuboidal – pancreas","Stratified squamous keratinised – skin","Transitional – bladder"],"e":"The <b>cornea</b> was an example of <b>simple squamous</b>; transitional was from the <b>bladder</b>.","w":["Correct.","Correct.","Correct.","Correct."],"k":"Cornea = simple squamous.","rn":[["","Image card: What does the arrow point to, and in which tissue?","What does the arrow point to, and in which tissue?","Simple squamous epithelium; Cornea; Single layer"],["","Where is simple squamous epithelium found (2 examples)?","Where is simple squamous epithelium found (2 examples)?","Kidney glomerulus; Cornea"],["","Where is transitional epithelium found (example)?","Where is transitional epithelium found (example)?","Bladder"]]},{"id":15072,"t":"Histology Techniques","s":"Which explanation best accounts for why fixation must happen soon after a biopsy is taken?","o":["Removed cells die fast; fixation preserves them","Removed cells divide fast, so fixation stops further division","Fixation stains tissue, so it must happen quickly","Fixation dehydrates tissue for paraffin","Fixation is optional for most samples"],"e":"Cells or tissue removed from the body <b>die very quickly</b>; formaldehyde fixation preserves them, <b>dead but preserved</b>, looking as they did when alive.","w":["They die.","Not.","Not.","Essential."],"k":"Fix quickly – cells die fast.","rn":[["","Why must tissue be fixed?","Why must tissue be fixed?","Removed cells die very quickly; Fixed cells are dead but look as they did alive"]]},{"id":15073,"t":"Histology Techniques","s":"Why would a cryotome be chosen over a standard microtome with paraffin?","o":["When the embedding medium is not paraffin","When sections thicker than 100 µm are needed","When the tissue has not been fixed","When cytology smears are needed","When the stain must be automated"],"e":"Sectioning uses a <b>microtome</b> or a <b>cryotome</b>; with a cryotome the <b>embedding medium is not paraffin</b>. Sections are still up to 10 μm.","w":["≤10 µm.","Not stated.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Cryotome: non-paraffin medium.","rn":[["","What is different about cryotome sectioning?","What is different about cryotome sectioning?","Embedding medium is not paraffin"]]},{"id":15074,"t":"Histology Techniques","s":"Which list contains only stains used in histology?","o":["H&E, trichrome, Golgi, silver, specific stains","H&E, trichrome, Gram and Ziehl–Neelsen stains","H&E only, for all tissues","Silver and gold only","Methylene blue and iodine"],"e":"Stains: <b>H&E</b>, <b>trichrome</b>, <b>Golgi</b>, <b>silver</b>, and other <b>specific tissue stains</b> (cartilage, bone, adipose).","w":["Not listed.","More.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Stain list.","rn":[["","What are the 5 types of stain listed?","What are the 5 types of stain listed?","H&E; Trichrome stain; Golgi stain; Silver stain; Other specific tissue stains"]]},{"id":15075,"t":"Histology Techniques","s":"A trainee claims the histopathologist is a lab technician who operates scanners. Which correction is accurate?","o":["They are doctors in a sub-specialty, using microscopes","They are technicians who process samples and operate the scanners","They are radiographers who image slides","They are doctors who no longer use microscopes","They are nurses who collect biopsies"],"e":"The <b>histopathologist is not a technician</b>: histopathology is a <b>sub-specialty of medicine</b>, and histopathologists still work best with their <b>microscope</b> (gold standard). Technicians process samples.","w":["Doctors.","Not.","Microscope.","Not."],"k":"Histopathologist = medical specialist.","rn":[["","Is the histopathologist a technician?","Is the histopathologist a technician?","No; A sub-specialty of medicine"]]},{"id":15076,"t":"Histology Techniques","s":"Which statement about the eosin component is correct?","o":["An acidic dye staining basic proteins pink","A basic dye staining acidic DNA pink","An acidic dye staining mucus pink","An acidic dye staining lipid pink","A basic dye staining cartilage purple"],"e":"<b>Eosin</b>: <b>acidic</b> dye → <b>basic/acidophilic</b> structures – cytoplasmic proteins, extracellular fibres; protein-rich secretions in ducts also stain. Pink = cytoplasm.","w":["Haematoxylin.","Unstained.","Unstained.","Haematoxylin."],"k":"Eosin = acidic dye, pink.","rn":[["","Eosin","Eosin","Acidic dye; Stains basic (acidophilic) structures; Pink"]]},{"id":15077,"t":"Tissue Types","s":"Which tissue would appear as purple dots (nuclei) scattered among wavy pink fibres with some thin dark wavy fibres?","o":["Fibrous connective tissue","Smooth muscle with collagen and elastic fibres","Transitional epithelium","Cardiac muscle","A nerve ganglion"],"e":"Fibrous connective tissue: <b>fibroblast</b> nuclei among <b>collagen</b> (broad pink) and <b>elastic</b> fibres (thin, dark, wavy).","w":["Spindle cells, not scattered dots.","Not.","Striated.","Not."],"k":"CT appearance.","rn":[["","Image card: What does this connective tissue show?","What does this connective tissue show?","Collagen fibres; Fibroblasts (dark nuclei)"],["","What are the 2 types of fibrous connective tissue?","What are the 2 types of fibrous connective tissue?","Loose; Dense"],["","What are the 3 components of fibrous tissue?","What are the 3 components of fibrous tissue?","Fibroblasts; Collagen fibres; Elastic fibres"]]},{"id":15078,"t":"Tissue Types","s":"In a pancreas section, a distinct rounded structure separate from surrounding specialised epithelium is seen. What is it most likely?","o":["A nerve ganglion","A goblet cell","An apoptotic body","A cartilage plate","A blood vessel"],"e":"Nerve ganglia appear as a <b>separate structure</b> from the surrounding tissue, embedded in connective tissue or among specialised epithelium such as the <b>pancreas</b>.","w":["Single cell.","Small.","Not.","Endothelium."],"k":"Ganglion = separate structure.","rn":[["","Image card: What is the arrow pointing to? Which organ?","What is the arrow pointing to? Which organ?","Nerve ganglion; Pancreas; Separate structure from surrounding tissue"],["","How can you identify a nerve ganglion?","How can you identify a nerve ganglion?","Looks like a separate structure from the surrounding tissue"]]},{"id":15079,"t":"Structure and Function","s":"An image shows a columnar epithelium in which some cells have dark granular cytoplasm and large nuclei and others are pale and vesicle-filled. What can you infer?","o":["Dark cells: protein-secreting; pale cells: mucin-secreting","Dark cells: mucin-secreting; pale cells: protein-secreting","Dark cells: dying; pale cells: healthy","Dark and pale cells: both ion-pumping","Dark and pale cells: both fibroblasts"],"e":"Appearance links to function: <b>protein-secreting</b> cells have <b>dark-stained cytoplasm</b> (high protein); <b>mucin-secreting</b> cells have pale <b>mucin vesicles</b>.","w":["Swapped.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Dark = protein; pale = mucin.","rn":[["","What do protein secreting cells look like?","What do protein secreting cells look like?","Very dark stained cytoplasm"],["","Image card: What type of epithelial cells are these? How can you tell?","What type of epithelial cells are these? How can you tell?","Protein secreting epithelial cells; Very dark stained cytoplasm (high protein)"],["","What are the 3 functional epithelial cell types?","What are the 3 functional epithelial cell types?","Mucin secreting; Ion pumping; Protein secreting"]]},{"id":15080,"t":"The Cell","s":"Which H&E finding would best support a cell undergoing mitosis rather than apoptosis?","o":["Two groups of condensed chromosomes pulling apart","A shrunken cell with blebbing","Membrane-enclosed fragments","Phagocytosed fragments in macrophages","Broken intercellular junctions with microvilli contraction"],"e":"Two chromosome groups pulling apart = <b>anaphase</b> (mitosis). Shrinkage, blebbing, fragments and phagocytosis are <b>apoptosis</b>.","w":["Apoptosis.","Apoptosis.","Apoptosis.","Apoptosis step 1."],"k":"Mitosis vs apoptosis on H&E."},{"id":15081,"t":"The Cell","s":"Where does chromatin condense during the second stage of apoptosis?","o":["Around the nuclear periphery","In the centre of the nucleus","In the cytoplasm","In the nucleolus only","Outside the cell"],"e":"Step 2: cell <b>shrinks</b>, chromatin condenses around the <b>nuclear periphery</b>.","w":["Periphery.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Apoptosis: peripheral chromatin.","rn":[["","Apoptosis step 2: what happens?","Apoptosis step 2: what happens?","Cell shrinks; Chromatin condenses around the nuclear edge"]]},{"id":15082,"t":"Structure and Function","s":"A lymph node biopsy comes back with this pale, washed-out appearance and no recognisable structure. What most likely went wrong?","o":["Poor or delayed fixation before processing","Sections cut too thin on the microtome","Haematoxylin applied for too long","Using a cryotome instead of a microtome","Imaging with whole slide imaging instead of a microscope"],"e":"Formaldehyde fixation cross-links proteins (CH₂ bridge, releasing water) to preserve structure. Poorly fixed tissue is pale and structureless and may be useless, risking a repeat biopsy.","w":["Thin sections (up to 10 μm) are correct for diagnosis.","Excess haematoxylin would make it too purple, not washed out.","A cryotome is a valid sectioning method.","Imaging method does not destroy tissue structure."],"k":"Washed-out structure = poor fixation.","img":{"src":"15/poorfix","cap":"Lymphoid tissue section after staining."}},{"id":15083,"t":"The Cell","s":"Which statement about H&E and organelles is correct?","o":["No organelles, but membrane, cytoplasm and nucleus are visible","Mitochondria and nucleus are visible, but not the membrane","Golgi and nucleus are visible, but not cytoplasm","Ribosomes and nucleolus are visible","Nothing of the cell is visible"],"e":"Only the <b>cell membrane, cytoplasm, nucleus/nucleoplasm (chromatin)</b> (and nucleolus) are identifiable; detailed organelles are not.","w":["Not.","Not.","Not.","Visible."],"k":"H&E limits.","rn":[["","What 4 things can be identified in a cell in histology?","What 4 things can be identified in a cell in histology?","Cell membrane; Cytoplasm; Nucleus / nucleoplasm; Chromatin"],["","Name 3 membrane-bound organelles in the cytoplasm.","Name 3 membrane-bound organelles in the cytoplasm.","Mitochondrion; Golgi apparatus; Lysosome"]]},{"id":15087,"t":"Summary","s":"Cells are scraped from the cervix, smeared and stained. Name this branch of pathology (cells rather than tissue sections).","e":"Cytology: cells smeared, stained and studied (e.g. cell scraping for cervical cancer screening); histology uses fixed, embedded tissue sections.","k":"Cytology.","a":["cytology","cytopathology"],"rn":[["","How is cytology done?","How is cytology done?","Cells smeared on a slide; Stained; Studied for abnormalities"]]},{"id":15088,"t":"Histology Techniques","s":"A surgeon removes part of an organ containing a tumour for histology. Name this type of biopsy.","e":"Surgical biopsy removes part of an organ; other methods: needle (FNA), punch, cell scraping (e.g. cervical screening).","k":"Surgical biopsy.","a":["surgical biopsy","surgical","excision biopsy","excisional biopsy","resection"],"rn":[["","What are the 3 types of biopsy?","What are the 3 types of biopsy?","Needle; Punch; Surgical"],["","What is a surgical biopsy used for?","What is a surgical biopsy used for?","Tumours; Large tissue; Part of an organ"]]},{"id":15090,"t":"Histology Techniques","s":"Which pair best describes the difference between histology and histopathology?","o":["Histology – normal; histopathology – diseased tissue","Histology – diseased; histopathology – normal tissue","Histology – cells; histopathology – organs","Histology – bones; histopathology – soft tissue","Both mean the same thing"],"e":"From the recording: <b>histology</b> = normal tissue; <b>histopathology</b> = diseased tissue.","w":["Swapped.","Tissues.","Not.","Different."],"k":"Histology normal; histopathology diseased.","rn":[["","What are the 5 uses of histology and histopathology?","What are the 5 uses of histology and histopathology?","Compare normal vs diseased tissue; Identify disease features at tissue and cell level; Link with symptoms and mechanism; Evaluate courses of treatment; Predict prognosis and progression"],["","Histopathology","Histopathology","Study of diseased tissue; An area of medicine"]]},{"id":15091,"t":"Histology Techniques","s":"Which explanation of “acidophilic” is correct?","o":["Basic structures taking up the acidic dye eosin","Acidic structures taking up the basic dye haematoxylin","Basic structures taking up the basic dye haematoxylin","DNA-rich structures taking up eosin","Structures that take up no dye"],"e":"<b>Acidophilic</b>: basic structures that take up the <b>acidic dye eosin</b> (cytoplasmic proteins, extracellular fibres).","w":["Basophilic.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Acidophilic = eosin-loving.","rn":[["","Eosin","Eosin","Acidic dye; Stains basic (acidophilic) structures; Pink"]]},{"id":15092,"t":"Tissue Types","s":"A slide shows tall cells in one layer with nuclei lined near the base and pale goblet cells interspersed. Which epithelium?","o":["Simple columnar with goblet cells","Pseudostratified columnar with goblet cells","Stratified columnar with goblet cells","Simple cuboidal with goblet cells","Transitional epithelium"],"e":"<b>Simple columnar</b>: one layer of tall cells with nuclei (N) lined up near the base, with <b>goblet cells (G)</b>.","w":["Nuclei aligned.","One layer.","Tall.","Not."],"k":"Simple columnar + goblet.","rn":[["","Image card: What type of epithelium is this? How can you tell?","What type of epithelium is this? How can you tell?","Simple columnar epithelium; Single layer of tall rectangular cells; N: nuclei"],["","Goblet Cells","Goblet Cells","Mucus-producing cells; In columnar epithelium"]]},{"id":15093,"t":"Tissue Types","s":"Which distinguishes pseudostratified from truly stratified epithelium on a section?","o":["Pseudostratified only looks layered, from nuclei at different heights","Pseudostratified has more true layers than stratified","Stratified only looks layered, from nuclei at different heights","Pseudostratified is keratinised; stratified is not","Stratified is a single layer"],"e":"<b>Pseudostratified</b>: epithelium that <b>looks layered</b> because nuclei are at different heights. <b>Stratified</b> genuinely has several layers (superficial, middle, basal).","w":["Not.","Swapped.","Not.","Several."],"k":"Pseudo = false layers.","rn":[["","Image card: What type of epithelium is this? How can you tell?","What type of epithelium is this? How can you tell?","Simple squamous epithelium; Single layer of flat cells; N: nuclei"],["","Image card: What type of epithelium is this? How can you tell?","What type of epithelium is this? How can you tell?","Simple cuboidal epithelium; Single layer of cube-like cells; N: nuclei"],["","When comparing normal and diseased tissue, what do you look for?","When comparing normal and diseased tissue, what do you look for?","What is the same; What is different"]]},{"id":15094,"t":"Tissue Types","s":"Which of these would most strongly suggest a section is from the bladder rather than the skin?","o":["Transitional, not keratinised, layers","Keratin on the surface of the epithelium","A simple squamous lining","Goblet cells in the lining","Striated muscle beneath"],"e":"The bladder has <b>transitional epithelium</b> (S, M, B layers); skin has <b>keratinised stratified squamous</b>.","w":["Skin.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Bladder vs skin.","rn":[["","What are the 3 layers of transitional epithelium?","What are the 3 layers of transitional epithelium?","S: superficial; M: middle; B: basal"]]},{"id":15097,"t":"Histology Techniques","s":"Which statement about cartilage on H&E and its staining logic is correct?","o":["Basophilic: haematoxylin stains carbohydrates","Acidophilic: eosin stains its carbohydrates","Basophilic: haematoxylin stains its collagen fibres","Unstained: carbohydrates do not take up dye","Acidophilic: it contains mucin vesicles"],"e":"Haematoxylin stains acidic/basophilic structures including <b>carbohydrates (cartilage molecules)</b>, so cartilage stains purple throughout.","w":["Haematoxylin.","Carbohydrates.","Carbohydrates stain.","Not."],"k":"Cartilage basophilic.","rn":[["","Haematoxylin","Haematoxylin","Basic dye; Stains acidic (basophilic) structures; Purple"],["","What is the exception to haematoxylin staining nuclei?","What is the exception to haematoxylin staining nuclei?","Cartilage; Whole tissue stains purple (carbohydrates)"],["","What does haematoxylin stain?","What does haematoxylin stain?","DNA (nucleus, nucleolus); RNA; Carbohydrates (cartilage molecules)"]]},{"id":15099,"t":"Tissue Types","s":"A student sees a tissue made of rounded cell profiles with central nuclei in sheets around a gut wall, without striation. Cut in another plane, they are spindle-shaped. What is it?","o":["Smooth muscle","Cardiac muscle","Stratified cuboidal epithelium","Dense collagen","Nerve ganglion"],"e":"Smooth muscle: <b>transverse</b> – rounded profiles, central nuclei; <b>longitudinal</b> – spindle cells, elongated nuclei; <b>no striation</b>.","w":["Striated.","Epithelium.","Fibres.","Not."],"k":"Smooth muscle planes.","rn":[["","Image card: What type of muscle is this? How can you tell?","What type of muscle is this? How can you tell?","Smooth muscle; No striation; Elongated nuclei"],["","Image card: What type of muscle is this? How can you tell?","What type of muscle is this? How can you tell?","Smooth muscle; No striation"]]},{"id":15100,"t":"Tissue Types","s":"The arrowed duct is lined by two layers of cube-shaped cells. Name this epithelium.","e":"Stratified cuboidal epithelium (two or more layers of cuboidal cells) lines ducts, e.g. an oesophageal gland duct.","k":"Stratified cuboidal.","img":{"src":"15/stratcub","cap":"Duct beside glands in the oesophageal wall, H&amp;E."},"a":["stratified cuboidal","stratified cuboidal epithelium"],"rn":[["","Image card: What type of epithelium is the arrow pointing to? Where is it from?","What type of epithelium is the arrow pointing to? Where is it from?","Stratified cuboidal epithelium; An oesophagus duct"],["","Where is stratified cuboidal epithelium found (example)?","Where is stratified cuboidal epithelium found (example)?","An oesophagus duct"]]},{"id":15101,"t":"Histology Techniques","s":"Which explanation best links section thickness to cytology vs histology?","o":["Both study individual cells; sections must be ≤10 µm thin","Both use smears; sections must be ≥100 µm thick","Cytology uses thick sections; histology uses smears","Thickness matters for cytology but not histology","Cytology samples are always paraffin-embedded"],"e":"In histology, single-cell-layer sections (≤10 μm) let you identify changes in individual cells. In cytology cells are <b>smeared</b> on a slide.","w":["Thin.","Swapped.","Histology.","Not."],"k":"Thin sections → individual cells.","rn":[["","Why do you want single cell layers?","Why do you want single cell layers?","Identify changes in individual cells; Overlapping layers are hard to interpret"],["","Cytology","Cytology","Study of cells; Also called cytopathology"],["","Why is correct embedding and thickness important?","Why is correct embedding and thickness important?","Single cell layer sections; For correct disease pathology diagnosis"]]},{"id":15102,"t":"Histology Techniques","s":"Which statement about why trichrome may be requested is correct?","o":["It shows layers more clearly for a specific question","It replaces H&E in routine diagnosis","It is the only stain that can be automated in the lab","It specifically stains lipid droplets","It is a basic dye like haematoxylin"],"e":"<b>Trichrome</b> (an acid combination stain) stains <b>different layers</b> much more clearly; may be requested for a <b>specific question</b>.","w":["H&E first.","H&E is automated.","Not.","Acid combination."],"k":"Trichrome: layers.","rn":[["","Trichrome Stain","Trichrome Stain","Acid combination stain; Stains the different tissue layers more clearly"]]},{"id":15103,"t":"Tissue Types","s":"Which epithelium would you look for lining the glomerulus of the kidney?","o":["Simple squamous","Transitional","Stratified squamous keratinised","Pseudostratified","Stratified cuboidal"],"e":"Kidney glomerulus: <b>simple squamous</b> epithelium.","w":["Bladder.","Skin.","Airway.","Duct."],"k":"Glomerulus: simple squamous.","rn":[["","Image card: What does the arrow point to, and in which organ?","What does the arrow point to, and in which organ?","Simple squamous epithelium; Kidney glomerulus"],["","Where is simple squamous epithelium found (2 examples)?","Where is simple squamous epithelium found (2 examples)?","Kidney glomerulus; Cornea"]]},{"id":15104,"t":"Tissue Types","s":"Which statement about where nerve ganglia sit is correct?","o":["In connective tissue or among pancreatic epithelium","Only within the brain and spinal cord, not peripheral organs","On the epithelial surface of the gut","Within red blood cells","Lining blood vessels"],"e":"Nerve ganglia are often <b>embedded in connective tissue</b>, or in areas such as the <b>pancreas</b>.","w":["Colon and pancreas.","Not.","Not.","Endothelium."],"k":"Ganglia location.","rn":[["","Where are nerve ganglia often found?","Where are nerve ganglia often found?","Embedded in connective tissue; Among specialised epithelium (e.g. pancreas)"]]},{"id":15107,"t":"Histology Techniques","s":"What safety point about the fixative is emphasised?","o":["Formaldehyde is carcinogenic","Formaldehyde is explosive","Paraffin is carcinogenic","Eosin is toxic","Haematoxylin is radioactive"],"e":"The fixation slide notes that <b>formaldehyde is carcinogenic</b>.","w":["Not.","Not stated.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Formaldehyde carcinogenic.","rn":[["","What is the safety warning about formaldehyde?","What is the safety warning about formaldehyde?","It is carcinogenic"]]},{"id":15108,"t":"Histology Techniques","s":"What is released when formaldehyde cross-links two proteins?","o":["Water (H₂O)","Carbon dioxide","Oxygen","Ammonia","Glucose"],"e":"Protein 1 (NH₂) + formaldehyde + protein 2 → cross-linked proteins joined by a <b>CH₂ bridge</b>, with <b>H₂O</b> released.","w":["Not.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Cross-linking releases water.","rn":[["","What does formaldehyde cross-linking release?","What does formaldehyde cross-linking release?","Water (H2O)"]]},{"id":15109,"t":"Histology Techniques","s":"Which statement about why understanding pathology at the cellular level helps clinicians is correct?","o":["Linking symptoms, guiding treatment and prognosis","Replacing the need to see or examine patients","Helping researchers, but not clinicians","Replacing blood tests in most diseases","Helping surgeons only"],"e":"Understanding how pathology emerges <b>at the cellular level</b> lets you <b>link symptoms</b>, make good <b>treatment judgements</b>, <b>advise</b> patients and <b>predict disease progression</b>.","w":["Not.","Clinicians.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Cellular pathology aids clinical judgement.","rn":[["","What are the 5 uses of histology and histopathology?","What are the 5 uses of histology and histopathology?","Compare normal vs diseased tissue; Identify disease features at tissue and cell level; Link with symptoms and mechanism; Evaluate courses of treatment; Predict prognosis and progression"],["","Why is understanding disease at cell level important?","Why is understanding disease at cell level important?","Link symptoms to what happens in the body; Good treatment judgements; Predict progression; Give better advice"]]},{"id":15110,"t":"Tissue Types","s":"Which tissue would be the best example of “fluid connective tissue”?","o":["Blood","Adipose","Cartilage","Areolar","Spongy bone"],"e":"Fluid connective tissue: <b>blood</b> (Heart, Lungs and Blood, Y1).","w":["Specialised.","Specialised.","Loose fibrous.","Specialised."],"k":"Blood = fluid CT.","rn":[["","What is the fluid connective tissue?","What is the fluid connective tissue?","Blood"]]},{"id":15111,"t":"Histology Techniques","s":"Which statement best describes the role of H&E in the stain sequence?","o":["H&E first; specific stains follow when needed","Specific stains first; H&E follows when needed","H&E only for research, not diagnosis","H&E after imaging","H&E is optional"],"e":"The <b>H&E stain always happens</b> first; others are more specific and laborious, used under specific instructions.","w":["Swapped.","Routine.","Before.","Always."],"k":"H&E first.","rn":[["","Why are other stains only used on request?","Why are other stains only used on request?","More specific; More laborious"]]},{"id":15112,"t":"Tissue Types","s":"This muscle shows striations, branching cells and central nuclei. Name the muscle type.","e":"Cardiac muscle cut longitudinally: notice the striation; cells branch and have central nuclei.","k":"Cardiac muscle.","img":{"src":"15/cardiac","cap":"Muscle, longitudinal section, H&amp;E."},"a":["cardiac","cardiac muscle","myocardium"],"rn":[["","What feature of cardiac muscle should you notice longitudinally?","What feature of cardiac muscle should you notice longitudinally?","Striation"],["","Image card: What type of muscle is this? What feature should you notice?","What type of muscle is this? What feature should you notice?","Cardiac muscle, longitudinal; Striation"],["","Image card: What type of muscle is this, and in which section?","What type of muscle is this, and in which section?","Cardiac muscle; Longitudinal"]]},{"id":15113,"t":"Tissue Types","s":"Name the epithelium indicated by the arrow, lining the capsule around this kidney glomerulus.","e":"Simple squamous epithelium: one layer of flat cells with flat nuclei, e.g. kidney glomerulus and cornea.","k":"Simple squamous.","img":{"src":"15/glom","cap":"Kidney cortex, H&amp;E. Arrow at the edge of the glomerulus."},"a":["simple squamous","simple squamous epithelium","squamous"],"rn":[["","Image card: What does the arrow point to, and in which organ?","What does the arrow point to, and in which organ?","Simple squamous epithelium; Kidney glomerulus"],["","Where is simple squamous epithelium found (2 examples)?","Where is simple squamous epithelium found (2 examples)?","Kidney glomerulus; Cornea"],["","Image card: What type of epithelium is this? How can you tell?","What type of epithelium is this? How can you tell?","Simple squamous epithelium; Single layer of flat cells; N: nuclei"]]},{"id":15114,"t":"The Cell","s":"In apoptosis, after the cell shrinks and then blebs violently, what is the next step?","o":["The cell breaks up into apoptotic bodies","The cell swells and bursts, releasing its contents","Microvilli contract and junctions break","Chromatin condenses at the nuclear periphery","The cell divides into two daughter cells"],"e":"Apoptosis steps: (1) microvilli contract, junctions break, chromatin begins to condense; (2) the cell shrinks and chromatin condenses at the nuclear periphery; (3) the cell blebs violently; (4) the cell fragments into apoptotic bodies, which are phagocytosed by neighbouring cells and roving macrophages.","w":["Apoptosis does not involve the cell bursting.","That is the first step.","That is the second step.","That is mitosis."],"k":"Recording coverage.","rn":[["","Apoptosis step 4: what does the cell break into?","Apoptosis step 4: what does the cell break into?","Membrane-enclosed apoptotic bodies"],["","Apoptosis step 5: what happens to apoptotic bodies?","Apoptosis step 5: what happens to apoptotic bodies?","Phagocytosed by neighbouring cells; And by roving macrophages"]]},{"id":15115,"t":"The Cell","s":"This cell shows two dark chromosome groups pulling apart to opposite poles within one cell. Name the mitotic stage.","e":"Anaphase: chromosomes separate to opposite poles. Order: prophase → metaphase (chromosomes in the middle) → anaphase → telophase (two nuclei forming, cell dividing).","k":"Anaphase.","img":{"src":"15/anaphase","cap":"Single cell, H&amp;E, high power."},"a":["anaphase"],"rn":[["","What are the 4 stages of mitosis seen on H&E?","What are the 4 stages of mitosis seen on H&E?","Prophase; Metaphase; Anaphase; Telophase"],["","Image card: What stage of mitosis is this?","What stage of mitosis is this?","Anaphase"]]},{"id":15116,"t":"The Cell","s":"This image shows two nuclei re-forming as the cell divides into two. Name the mitotic stage.","e":"Telophase: nuclei re-form at each pole and the cell divides. It follows anaphase.","k":"Telophase.","img":{"src":"15/telophase","cap":"Cells, H&amp;E, high power."},"a":["telophase","telophase and cytokinesis","cytokinesis"],"rn":[["","Image card: What stage of mitosis is this?","What stage of mitosis is this?","Telophase"]]},{"id":15117,"t":"Tissue Types","s":"This tissue from a gut wall has spindle-shaped cells with central elongated nuclei and no striations. Name the muscle type.","e":"Smooth muscle: spindle cells with central nuclei, no striations; cut across, profiles are rounded with central nuclei.","k":"Smooth muscle.","img":{"src":"15/smooth","cap":"Muscle, two planes of section, H&amp;E."},"a":["smooth","smooth muscle","visceral muscle"],"rn":[["","Image card: What type of muscle is this? How can you tell?","What type of muscle is this? How can you tell?","Smooth muscle; No striation; Elongated nuclei"],["","Image card: What type of muscle is this? How can you tell?","What type of muscle is this? How can you tell?","Smooth muscle; No striation"]]},{"id":15118,"t":"Histology Techniques","s":"Which statement about collection of cells by fluid aspiration is correct?","o":["Fluid aspirates collect a number of cells for analysis","Fluid aspirates collect whole organs for analysis","Fluid aspirates are used only for skin","Fluid aspirates require surgery","Fluid aspirates cannot be stained"],"e":"From the recording: <b>fluid aspirates</b> also collect a number of cells; cell scraping gets a few cells; surgical biopsies remove tissue/organ parts.","w":["Not.","Lumps, fluid.","Needle.","They can."],"k":"Aspirates collect cells.","rn":[["","What are the 2 main ways to collect samples?","What are the 2 main ways to collect samples?","Cell scraping; Biopsy"],["","What 2 things can be collected?","What 2 things can be collected?","Cells; Tissues"]]},{"id":15119,"t":"Tissue Types","s":"Which combination would most likely identify an artery in a pancreas section?","o":["Endothelium-lined lumen, smooth muscle wall, red cells","Endothelium-lined lumen, skeletal muscle wall, red cells","Goblet cells and cilia","Keratin and S/M/B layers","Mucin vesicles and acini"],"e":"Blood vessels: <b>endothelium</b>, surrounding <b>smooth muscle</b>, <b>red blood cells</b>.","w":["Smooth.","Airway.","Skin.","Gland."],"k":"Vessel identification.","rn":[["","How can blood vessels be identified?","How can blood vessels be identified?","Red blood cells inside; Endothelium lining; Surrounding smooth muscle"],["","Image card: What is this structure? What do the black, blue and green arrows show?","What is this structure? What do the black, blue and green arrows show?","Blood vessel; Black: endothelium; Blue: surrounding smooth muscle; Green: red blood cells"]]},{"id":15120,"t":"Histology Techniques","s":"A biopsy was left too long before reaching fixative. Why might its H&E slide be poor?","o":["Unfixed tissue stains poorly, losing structure","Unfixed tissue stains far too strongly, hiding the nuclei","Unfixed tissue cannot be cut thinner than 25 µm","Unfixed tissue turns into a cytology smear","Unfixed tissue keeps dividing on the slide"],"e":"Cells removed from the body die very quickly; fixation preserves them in a life-like state. Tissue that is not fixed properly will not stain properly, so structures are lost and diagnosis may be incomplete – possibly needing a repeat biopsy.","w":["Poorly.","Not stated.","Not.","Cells die."],"k":"Lecturer contact.","rn":[["","Image card: Which tissue is well fixed and which is poorly fixed?","Which tissue is well fixed and which is poorly fixed?","Left: poorly fixed (stains badly, nothing identifiable); Right: well fixed"],["","What happens if a sample is poorly fixed?","What happens if a sample is poorly fixed?","Does not stain properly; Cannot be analysed; Can lead to incomplete diagnosis"]]},{"id":15121,"t":"Histology Techniques","s":"A surgeon removes a breast lump and sends it to the laboratory. The specimen is left on a bench for several hours before it is put into formaldehyde. When the slides are made, the cells look poorly preserved and the pathologist cannot reach a full diagnosis.<br><br>What went wrong, and how does formaldehyde normally preserve tissue?","o":["Fixation was delayed; formaldehyde cross-links proteins to keep tissue life-like","Staining was too long; formaldehyde stains nuclei purple","Sectioning was too thin; formaldehyde makes sections 10 μm thick","Embedding failed; formaldehyde replaces the paraffin wax","Fixation was too fast; formaldehyde dissolves the cell proteins so they can be seen more clearly"],"e":"Step 1: fixation preserves the sample in a life-like state, and insufficient fixation makes the slide very difficult to study and can lead to an incomplete diagnosis. Step 2: formaldehyde fixes tissue by cross-linking proteins (water is released); it is also carcinogenic, so it is handled carefully.","w":["Formaldehyde is a fixative, not a stain; haematoxylin stains nuclei purple.","Section thickness is set by the microtome, not formaldehyde.","Formaldehyde does not replace paraffin; embedding is a later step.","Formaldehyde cross-links proteins rather than dissolving them."],"k":"Poor fixation → incomplete diagnosis; formaldehyde cross-links proteins.","rn":[["","How does formaldehyde work?","How does formaldehyde work?","Cross-links proteins together; Sticks proteins together"],["","Why is fixation essential?","Why is fixation essential?","Preserves the sample in a life-like state"],["","Fixation","Fixation","Sample treatment; Preserves the sample in a life-like state"]]},{"id":15122,"t":"Histology Techniques","s":"Three patients need samples. Patient 1 has a fluid-filled lump in the neck. Patient 2 has an itchy patch of skin that needs a small core of skin examined. Patient 3 is having part of an organ removed because of a large tumour. A fourth person attends routine cervical screening.<br><br>Which collection method suits each?","o":["1: fine needle aspiration; 2: punch biopsy; 3: surgical biopsy; 4: cell scraping","1: punch biopsy; 2: cell scraping; 3: fine needle aspiration; 4: surgical biopsy","1: surgical biopsy; 2: fine needle aspiration; 3: punch biopsy; 4: cell scraping","1: cell scraping; 2: surgical biopsy; 3: punch biopsy; 4: fine needle aspiration","All four: surgical biopsy, as it is the only method that gives a single cell layer section"],"e":"Step 1: needle biopsy (fine needle aspiration, FNA) investigates fluid samples, lumps and masses, and punch biopsy is used for skin investigation. Step 2: surgical biopsy investigates tumours, large tissue or part of an organ, and cell scraping is used for skin cells and cervical cancer screening.","w":["A fluid lump needs a needle (FNA), and skin needs a punch.","A large tumour needs a surgical biopsy.","Cervical screening uses cell scraping, not a needle.","Different samples suit different methods."],"k":"FNA: fluid/lumps; punch: skin; surgical: tumours/organs; scraping: cervical/skin cells.","rn":[["","What are the 3 types of biopsy?","What are the 3 types of biopsy?","Needle; Punch; Surgical"],["","Fine Needle Aspiration is an example of what?","Fine Needle Aspiration is an example of what?","Needle biopsy"],["","FNA","FNA","Fine Needle Aspiration"]]},{"id":15123,"t":"Histology Techniques","s":"A trainee cuts paraffin-embedded tissue sections that are much thicker than usual. Under the microscope, several layers of cells overlap and the pathologist cannot judge the cells clearly. In a second case, a surgeon needs a section from tissue that has not been embedded in paraffin.<br><br>What is the problem with the thick sections, and which instrument is used for the second case?","o":["Sections over 10 μm lose the single cell layer; a cryotome","Sections are too thin to stain; a microtome only","Thick sections fix poorly; a scanner for whole slide imaging","Thick sections stain only pink; a fine needle","Thick sections are needed for diagnosis; a microtome, because a cryotome uses paraffin wax"],"e":"Step 1: tissue is sectioned up to 10 μm thick, and correct embedding and section thickness ensure single cell layer sections for correct pathology diagnosis; thicker sections cause cells to overlap. Step 2: a microtome cuts paraffin-embedded sections, while a cryotome is used when the embedding medium is not paraffin.","w":["The sections were too thick, not too thin.","Fixation happens before embedding; WSI is imaging, not sectioning.","Thickness does not change which dye binds.","Single cell layers are needed, and a cryotome uses a non-paraffin medium."],"k":"Sections ≤10 μm for single cell layers; cryotome for non-paraffin tissue.","rn":[["","Why is correct embedding and thickness important?","Why is correct embedding and thickness important?","Single cell layer sections; For correct disease pathology diagnosis"],["","What is different about cryotome sectioning?","What is different about cryotome sectioning?","Embedding medium is not paraffin"]]},{"id":15124,"t":"The Cell","s":"A pathologist looks at a tumour section stained with H&E. In one cell, dark condensed chromosomes are lined up across the middle. In a neighbouring cell, two dark groups of chromosomes are pulling apart to opposite ends. A third cell shows two separate nuclei forming.<br><br>Which stages of mitosis are these, in order?","o":["Metaphase, anaphase, telophase","Prophase, metaphase, anaphase","Anaphase, telophase, prophase","Telophase, anaphase, metaphase","Prophase, telophase, metaphase, as chromosomes condense last of all"],"e":"Step 1: in metaphase, dark condensed chromosomes gather in the middle of the cell, and in anaphase two dark chromosome groups pull apart. Step 2: in telophase, two separate nuclei form; prophase comes earlier, with chromosomes condensing inside the nucleus.","w":["Prophase shows chromosomes condensing inside the nucleus, not lined up.","The first cell is metaphase, not anaphase.","The order is reversed.","Chromosomes condense in prophase, the first stage."],"k":"Metaphase (middle) → anaphase (apart) → telophase (two nuclei).","rn":[["","What are the 4 stages of mitosis seen on H&E?","What are the 4 stages of mitosis seen on H&E?","Prophase; Metaphase; Anaphase; Telophase"]]},{"id":15125,"t":"The Cell","s":"In a section of normal intestine, a pathologist sees a few cells that have shrunk and whose surfaces bulge outwards. Nearby, small membrane-enclosed fragments are being engulfed by neighbouring cells and by roving macrophages. There is no sign of the cells bursting.<br><br>What process is shown, and what are the fragments?","o":["Apoptosis; apoptotic bodies being phagocytosed","Mitosis; daughter cells in telophase","Necrosis; leaked cytoplasm","Fixation artefact; folded tissue that overlaps neighbouring cells","Holocrine secretion; whole cells releasing their product"],"e":"Step 1: in apoptosis the cell shrinks, blebs and fragments into apoptotic bodies. Step 2: apoptotic bodies are membrane-enclosed fragments that are phagocytosed by neighbouring cells and roving macrophages.","w":["Telophase shows two nuclei forming, not shrinking blebbing cells.","The cells have not burst or leaked.","Artefacts do not produce shrinking cells and engulfed fragments.","Holocrine secretion is a gland mode, not this cell death pattern."],"k":"Apoptosis: shrink, bleb, apoptotic bodies → phagocytosed.","rn":[["","Apoptosis step 5: what happens to apoptotic bodies?","Apoptosis step 5: what happens to apoptotic bodies?","Phagocytosed by neighbouring cells; And by roving macrophages"]]},{"id":15126,"t":"Tissue Types","s":"A student scanning a virtual slide of the stomach wall sees a round space lined by a thin layer of flat cells. Inside the space are small pink discs, and around it is a layer of spindle-shaped cells with elongated nuclei. Her tutor asks her to name the structure and its layers.<br><br>What is the structure, and which features identify it?","o":["A blood vessel: endothelium, red blood cells in the lumen and surrounding smooth muscle","A gland: goblet cells, mucin and myoepithelial cells","A nerve ganglion: neurons, glia and a capsule","A duct: stratified cuboidal epithelium only","A lymph node: lymphocytes, a capsule and reticular fibres lining the space"],"e":"Step 1: a blood vessel is lined by endothelium (a thin layer of flat cells) and can be identified by red blood cells in the lumen. Step 2: blood vessels can have surrounding smooth muscle tissue, matching the spindle cells with elongated nuclei.","w":["There are no goblet cells or mucin described.","There are no neuron cell bodies described.","The lining is thin and flat, with red cells inside.","The space contains red blood cells, not lymphocytes."],"k":"Blood vessel: endothelium + RBCs + smooth muscle.","rn":[["","How can blood vessels be identified?","How can blood vessels be identified?","Red blood cells inside; Endothelium lining; Surrounding smooth muscle"],["","Image card: What is this structure? What do the black, blue and green arrows show?","What is this structure? What do the black, blue and green arrows show?","Blood vessel; Black: endothelium; Blue: surrounding smooth muscle; Green: red blood cells"]]},{"id":15127,"t":"Histology Techniques","s":"A patient has two tests. In the first, cells are brushed from the cervix, smeared onto a slide, stained and checked for abnormalities. In the second, a piece of tissue is removed from a lump, fixed, embedded, sectioned, stained and imaged.<br><br>Which disciplines are these, and what is the correct order of steps for the second?","o":["Cytology, then histology: collect, preserve, prepare, stain, image and analyse","Histology, then cytology: stain, fix, collect, image","Both histology: image, stain, fix, collect","Cytology, then histology: collect, stain, fix, embed, image","Both cytology, because both samples end up stained on a glass slide and are imaged under a microscope"],"e":"Step 1: cytology (cytopathology) is the study of cells, where cells are smeared on a slide, stained and studied, while histology (histopathology) is the study of tissues. Step 2: the histology process is sample collection, sample preservation (fixation), sample preparation (embedding, sectioning), sample staining, then imaging and analysis.","w":["The labels are reversed and the steps are out of order.","The first test studies cells, so it is cytology.","Fixation (preservation) comes before staining.","The second test studies a piece of tissue, so it is histology."],"k":"Cytology = cells; histology = tissues: collect → preserve → prepare → stain → image.","rn":[["","How do you remember the histology process?","How do you remember the histology process?","Collect, Preserve, Prepare, Stain, Image; MNEMONIC"]]},{"id":15128,"t":"Summary","s":"A student is given an H&E image labelled \"pancreas\". At low magnification she sees epithelium and connective tissue. At higher magnification she sees cells packed with pink granules that look like protein-secreting cells. One region, however, has disorganised cells with abnormal nuclei.<br><br>Following the lecture's approach, what should she do next?","o":["Ask what has changed and predict the resulting change in function","Ignore it, as sections often show damage","Restain with Golgi stain before any analysis","Start again at low magnification without using the organ source","Assume it is normal, because cells never look as flawless as in textbooks and any change is an artefact"],"e":"Step 1: the approach is: note the organ source, identify tissues at low magnification, confirm cell types at higher magnification, link arrangement to function, and ask whether features look as expected. Step 2: if not, ask why and what has changed, and predict the change in function; disorganised cells with abnormal nuclei are not normal, so she should do this.","w":["Artefacts exist, but abnormal cells should still be assessed.","The stain choice depends on the aim; the next step is interpretation.","She has already done the low-power step and used the source.","Artefacts do not explain disorganised cells with abnormal nuclei."],"k":"Histology approach: source → low → high power → function → normal? → what changed?","rn":[["","If features look abnormal, what do you ask?","If features look abnormal, what do you ask?","Why?; What has changed?; Predicted change in function"],["","What are the 7 steps to analyse a histology image?","What are the 7 steps to analyse a histology image?","Know the organ/tissue source; Low magnification: tissue types; Higher magnification: cell types and arrangement; Link cells, arrangement and function; Does it look normal?; If not, what has changed?; Predicted change in function"]]},{"id":15129,"t":"Summary","s":"A student reviewing a respiratory slide finds that the cilia look like a blurred stain rather than clear hairs. The epithelium has a break in it, some tissue folds overlap, and a blood vessel contains no red blood cells. She worries that the patient has several diseases.<br><br>Which statement best addresses her concern?","o":["These are common section artefacts; real sections are not as flawless as textbook images","Each finding proves disease, as normal tissue never shows these","The tissue must have been stained with silver stain","Blurred cilia always mean ciliary disease","Vessels without red blood cells are always arteries that have been blocked by a clot"],"e":"Step 1: histology sections may show some \"damage\", and cells or tissues may not look as flawless as in textbooks. Step 2: examples include cilia looking like a blurred stain, breaks in the epithelium, overlapping folds, vessels without red blood cells and pale staining, so these findings may be artefacts rather than disease.","w":["The lecture lists these as typical artefacts.","The findings are artefacts, not caused by stain type.","Blurred cilia are a listed artefact.","Vessels without red cells are a listed artefact."],"k":"Expect artefacts: blurred cilia, breaks, folds, empty vessels, pale stain.","rn":[["","Why may cells not look like the textbook?","Why may cells not look like the textbook?","Histology sections may show damage"]]},{"id":15130,"t":"Tissue Types","s":"A student reviews slides from four organs. Slide A, from the oesophagus, shows many layers of flat cells without keratin. Slide B, from the kidney glomerulus, shows a single layer of flat cells. Slide C, from the bladder, shows layered cells with superficial, middle and basal layers. Slide D, from the pancreas, shows a single layer of cube-shaped cells.<br><br>Which epithelia are these?","o":["A: non-keratinised stratified squamous; B: simple squamous; C: transitional; D: simple cuboidal","A: keratinised stratified squamous; B: simple cuboidal; C: pseudostratified; D: simple columnar","A: transitional; B: simple squamous; C: stratified cuboidal; D: simple squamous","A: simple columnar; B: pseudostratified; C: transitional; D: stratified squamous","A: non-keratinised stratified squamous; B: pseudostratified ciliated; C: stratified squamous; D: transitional"],"e":"Step 1: the oesophagus is lined by non-keratinised stratified squamous epithelium, and the kidney glomerulus by simple squamous epithelium. Step 2: the bladder has transitional epithelium with superficial, middle and basal layers, and the pancreas has simple cuboidal epithelium.","w":["The oesophagus is non-keratinised, and the glomerulus is squamous.","The oesophagus is stratified squamous, not transitional.","The oesophagus is stratified, and the glomerulus is simple squamous.","The glomerulus is simple squamous and the bladder transitional."],"k":"Oesophagus NK stratified squamous; glomerulus simple squamous; bladder transitional; pancreas cuboidal.","rn":[["","Where is non-keratinised stratified squamous epithelium?","Where is non-keratinised stratified squamous epithelium?","Oesophagus"],["","Image card: What type of epithelium is this? Where is it from?","What type of epithelium is this? Where is it from?","Stratified squamous epithelium, non-keratinised; Oesophagus"]]}]);
